{"reference":"Amos 2","book":"Amos","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Accusations","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The repeated “three transgressions … and four” formula presents wrongdoing as accumulated and full; it is not a literal count of four offenses. The sequence of judgments through the nations reaches its climax in Israel, though the idea that an original audience would have cheered the earlier indictments remains a reconstruction rather than a certainty. Israel’s charges focus on exploitation of vulnerable people, while the brief sexual accusation in verse 7 is textually difficult and must not be used to blame victims or excuse coercion. The closing judgment language is severe; it calls for lament and ethical scrutiny, not for readers to claim permission for vengeance or punishment.","verse_refs":["Amos 2:1-16","Amos 2:1-3","Amos 2:6-8","Amos 2:13-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_amos_002_5f4fbb2e416813f1bbcbff20","reader_path_amos_002_69be956df7105579766d3dec"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}